CAT = "ukaplhPremises"; SEG = "PRM"
Q = [
("To which licensing authority do you apply for a premises licence?",
 "Where the premises are", ["Where the applicant lives", "Where the company is registered", "Any authority you choose"],
 "A premises licence application goes to the licensing authority for the area where the premises are situated. A personal licence is the one tied to where you live."),
("What is the minimum age for an individual to apply for a premises licence?",
 "18", ["16", "21", "25"],
 "An individual applicant must be at least 18 and must be carrying on, or proposing to carry on, a business that involves using the premises for licensable activities."),
("Which document must accompany every premises licence application?",
 "An operating schedule", ["A five-year business plan", "A marketing and pricing plan", "A weekly staff rota"],
 "The application must include an operating schedule, a plan of the premises and, if alcohol will be sold, the proposed DPS's written consent. The operating schedule sets out how the premises will run."),
("For how long must the notice of a new premises licence application be displayed at the premises?",
 "28 consecutive days", ["10 working days", "14 consecutive days", "21 consecutive days"],
 "The notice must be displayed for 28 consecutive days starting the day after the application is given to the authority, the same as the period for representations."),
("What colour paper must the notice of a premises licence application be printed on?",
 "Pale blue", ["White", "Yellow", "Pale green"],
 "The regulations require an A4 or larger notice on pale blue paper, printed legibly in at least 16 point type, placed where it can be read from outside."),
("Where else must a new premises licence application be advertised?",
 "In a local newspaper", ["On local radio", "In the London Gazette", "In the parish newsletter"],
 "As well as the blue notice at the premises, the application must be advertised in a local newspaper or similar publication circulating near the premises."),
("Which detail must an operating schedule include?",
 "Hours of licensable activities", ["The expected annual turnover", "The price list for drinks", "The names of all bar staff"],
 "The operating schedule sets out the licensable activities, the times they take place, other opening hours, the DPS, whether alcohol is for on or off sale and the steps taken to promote the licensing objectives."),
("A premises licence application gets no relevant representations. What must the authority do?",
 "Grant it as applied for", ["Hold a hearing anyway", "Refer it to the police", "Grant it for one year"],
 "Where no relevant representations are made, the authority must grant the licence on the terms in the operating schedule, plus any mandatory conditions. A hearing is only needed when there are representations."),
("Unless it is granted for a limited time, how long does a premises licence last?",
 "Indefinitely", ["For one year", "For five years", "For ten years"],
 "A premises licence lasts until it is revoked, surrendered or lapses, for example on the death or insolvency of the holder. It can be granted for a set period, such as for a festival."),
("What is the annual fee for a premises licence based on?",
 "Non-domestic rateable value", ["The number of staff employed", "The opening hours applied for", "The volume of alcohol sold"],
 "Premises fees are set in bands A to E by the premises' non-domestic rateable value, with a higher multiplier for the largest venues used mainly for drinking."),
("What must be displayed prominently at licensed premises?",
 "The premises licence summary", ["The full premises licence", "The staff training records", "The latest refusals log"],
 "The holder must keep the full licence, or a certified copy, at the premises and display the summary prominently, with a notice naming the person who has custody of the licence."),
("Which document shows the layout of the premises, including exits and bar areas?",
 "The premises plan", ["The licence summary", "The fire certificate", "The refusals log"],
 "A plan of the premises in the prescribed form must be submitted with the application, showing features such as exits, bars, stages and toilets."),
("When must on-licensed premises provide free tap water?",
 "When a customer asks", ["Only with a paid meal", "Only after 11 pm", "Only to designated drivers"],
 "A mandatory condition requires free potable water to be provided on request where reasonably available. It applies whatever the time or whether food is served."),
("Which spirit measures must be available under the mandatory conditions?",
 "25 ml or 35 ml", ["20 ml or 30 ml", "50 ml only", "25 ml or 50 ml"],
 "Gin, rum, vodka and whisky must be available in 25 ml or 35 ml measures. Customers must be told that these small measures are available."),
("What is the smallest glass of still wine that must be available?",
 "125 ml", ["100 ml", "150 ml", "175 ml"],
 "Still wine must be available in a 125 ml glass, and customers must be made aware of it on menus, price lists or by staff."),
("What is the small measure of beer or cider that must be available?",
 "Half a pint", ["A third of a pint", "Two-thirds of a pint", "A quarter of a pint"],
 "Beer and cider sold on draught for drinking on the premises must be available in half pints."),
("Which of these is an irresponsible promotion banned by the mandatory conditions?",
 "Unlimited drinks for a fixed fee", ["A half price soft drinks offer", "A free glass of water on request", "Half price food before 7 pm"],
 "Promotions that encourage people to drink quickly or excessively, such as drink as much as you like for a set price, are banned. Free water on request is itself a mandatory condition."),
("Under the mandatory age verification condition, ID must show a photograph, a date of birth and what else?",
 "A holographic or UV mark", ["The holder's home address", "The holder's signature", "An issue and expiry date"],
 "Acceptable ID must bear the holder's photograph, date of birth and either a holographic mark or an ultraviolet feature, such as a passport, photocard driving licence or PASS card."),
("Who must ensure that alcohol is supplied in line with the premises' age verification policy?",
 "The designated premises supervisor", ["The licensed door supervisor", "The licensing authority officer", "The most senior bartender"],
 "The premises licence holder must make sure a policy is adopted, and the DPS must ensure that sales follow it. Bar staff carry out the checks under that policy."),
("Under the mandatory conditions, alcohol must not be sold below what price?",
 "Duty plus VAT", ["The trade cost price", "Half the shelf price", "The local average price"],
 "The ban on below-cost sales sets the floor at the alcohol duty plus VAT on that duty. It applies to on and off sales."),
("A pub wants to permanently extend its alcohol hours late into the night. What should it apply for?",
 "A full variation", ["A minor variation", "A licence transfer", "A provisional statement"],
 "Extending the hours for alcohol cannot be done by minor variation, so a full variation is needed, with the same notices and consultation as a new application."),
("What can a minor variation never be used for?",
 "Extending alcohol sale hours", ["Changing the layout slightly", "Removing an outdated condition", "Adding a volunteered condition"],
 "Minor variations are for changes that could not undermine the objectives. They cannot extend the time for sale of alcohol, add the sale of alcohol, or vary the DPS."),
("Which of these can object to the transfer of a premises licence?",
 "The police", ["Local residents", "Trading standards", "The fire authority"],
 "On a transfer, only the police, and on immigration grounds the Home Office, can object. Residents and other responsible authorities have no say in who holds an existing licence."),
("A premises licence holder dies. Within how many days of the lapse must an interim authority notice be given?",
 "28 days", ["7 days", "14 days", "21 days"],
 "The licence lapses on the holder's death, incapacity or insolvency, but an interim authority notice given within 28 days of the lapse reinstates it. The period was seven days before 2010."),
("Which of these people can apply for a review of a premises licence?",
 "A nearby resident", ["Only a magistrate", "Only the DPS", "Only the landlord"],
 "Any responsible authority or any other person, such as a resident or local business, can apply for a review if the premises are undermining the licensing objectives."),
("On a review, what is the longest a licensing authority can suspend a premises licence?",
 "Three months", ["One month", "Six months", "Twelve months"],
 "After a review the authority may modify conditions, exclude an activity, remove the DPS, suspend the licence for up to three months or revoke it."),
("Which step can a licensing authority take after reviewing a premises licence?",
 "Remove the DPS", ["Fine the holder", "Jail the DPS", "Ban the customers"],
 "Review powers are about the licence: changing conditions, excluding activities, removing the DPS, suspension or revocation. Fines and prison come only from a criminal court."),
("What triggers a summary review of a premises licence?",
 "Serious crime or disorder", ["Repeated noise complaints", "An unpaid annual fee", "A change of the DPS"],
 "A police superintendent or above can certify that premises are associated with serious crime or serious disorder. This starts the fast-track summary review process."),
("In a summary review, how quickly must the licensing authority consider interim steps?",
 "Within 48 hours", ["Within 24 hours", "Within 7 days", "Within 28 days"],
 "The authority must consider interim steps, such as suspension, within 48 hours of receiving the application, and hold the full review hearing within 28 days."),
("What happens if a premises licence holder does not pay the annual fee?",
 "The authority suspends it", ["It lapses permanently", "The DPS is fined", "The police close the venue"],
 "The licensing authority must suspend the licence for non-payment of the annual fee, after giving notice. The suspension ends when the fee is paid."),
("Premises are still being built. What can a developer seek to find out if a licence would be granted?",
 "A provisional statement", ["A temporary event notice", "A minor variation", "An interim authority notice"],
 "A provisional statement can be applied for while premises are being built or altered. Representations are then limited once the work is finished and a licence is applied for."),
("A premises licence holder changes its registered name. What must it do?",
 "Notify the licensing authority", ["Apply for a new licence", "Display a new blue notice", "Tell only the local police"],
 "The holder must notify the authority of a change of name or address and send the licence so it can be updated. No new licence or notice period is needed."),
]
